# Cognitive & token efficiency

- Prefer minimal diffs. Do not rewrite whole files for small edits; skip unchanged sections with `// ... existing code ...` (or `# ... existing code ...` in PHP/Python).
- Be concise. Explain code only when asked. No filler intros, outros, or restating the task.
- Reply with only the changed snippet/diff unless a short clarification is required to proceed.

# Code safety & continuity

- Never delete, rename, or change existing functions/variables unless the user explicitly asks.
- Do not break current architecture, APIs, or state management.
- Reuse existing helpers/utilities before adding new ones.
- Do not add convenience files, scripts, or docs the user did not ask for.
- Do not touch `Credentials.txt` or `WhatsApp Image 2026-11-10 at 2.15.38 PM.jpeg` unless the user explicitly allows it. Ask first if their info is needed.

# Jadeed Rifah Rehab ERP stack (do not rewrite)

- Frontend: React (responsive UI + PWA installable app)
- Backend: Laravel (REST API)
- Database: MySQL
- File uploads: store on the same server (local storage), not Cloudinary by default
- Hosting target: cPanel-style VPS/shared (domain/subdomain + SSL)
- Follow SRS modules/roles/workflows; do not invent scope (no live CCTV control, no in-app camera scanning unless asked)
- Build phase-by-phase; do not jump ahead unless asked

# Language

- English only for all user-facing UI text.
- Do not add Roman Urdu, language toggle, or i18n unless the user explicitly asks later.
- Keep terminology consistent across modules.

# UI clarity (mandatory)

- Keep UI clean and uncluttered. No messy layouts.
- Do NOT add useless helper text, hint lines, tips, placeholders-as-instructions, or AI-style “helpful” microcopy that confuses users.
- Labels should be short and clear. Only show helper text when it is truly required for a critical action.
- Prefer one clear action per area; avoid extra badges, chips, and decorative clutter.

# Feedback UI — toast & modal (production-grade)

- Do NOT use browser `alert()`, `confirm()`, or `prompt()` as the default UX. They look unprofessional / testing-phase.
- Use a shared **toast/toaster** for success, error, info, and light warnings.
- Use a shared **React modal/dialog** for confirmations, destructive actions, and forms that need focus.
- `alert`/`confirm`/`prompt` only where truly necessary (rare); never sprinkle them across the app.
- Where a toast or modal fits, use toast/modal — not native browser dialogs.
- Keep one consistent toast + modal system app-wide (do not mix random libraries per screen).

# Responsive & adaptive UI (mandatory on EVERY UI task)

- Never ship UI that only works on desktop. Every screen/component must work on mobile, tablet, and laptop/desktop without the user re-prompting.
- Use responsive layout by default (flex/grid + breakpoints). No fixed widths that break on small screens.
- Use a consistent spacing scale for margin/padding. Do not guess random values.
- Align buttons, inputs, labels, and actions properly; no uneven gaps or misaligned controls.
- Buttons must have consistent height, padding, and alignment across the app.
- Text, tables, forms, sidebars, and modals must adapt (wrap/stack/scroll sensibly) on narrow screens.
- Before finishing any UI work, self-check: alignment, overflow, wrapping, tap targets, and spacing on small and large screens.
- Prefer shared UI components over one-off spacing hacks.

# Growing lists — scroll + search (mandatory)

- If a list/table can grow over time (patients, admissions, staff, visitors, medicines, inventory, payments, fees due), do NOT dump the full list as an endlessly long page.
- Put growing data in a **fixed-height panel with inner scroll** so header/actions/forms stay visible and the user does not scroll the whole app forever.
- Add a simple **search** (name/code/phone as relevant) on those expandable lists; debounce search input.
- Apply this on every new or touched list UI without waiting for the user to re-ask.
- Track status in `OPTIMIZATION.md` items 13 (inner scroll) and 14 (list search).

# Performance habits (alongside development)

Must apply while building features (not only at the end):
- Frontend lazy loading for routes/modules
- Skeleton/shimmer loading for async UI
- Database indexes on FKs/search/filter columns
- Slim API responses (only required fields)
- Avoid N+1 queries (use Laravel eager loading)
- Inner scroll panels for growing lists/tables
- Search on expandable lists (debounced)

Should apply at a light level while building; deepen later:
- Light caching where clearly useful
- Debounced search/filters (also required on new expandable lists — see Must)
- Image/file upload limits + basic optimization
- React code splitting by module
- Default pagination page sizes (e.g. 10/20/50)

# Security habits (alongside development)

Must apply while building features (code/app security):
- Auth on every protected API
- RBAC/permission checks per action
- Backend input validation
- ORM/prepared queries only (no SQL injection)
- XSS prevention
- Password hashing
- Secrets only in `.env`
- Mass assignment protection
- Secure uploads (type/size whitelist)
- HTTPS + secure cookies/tokens in production
- Session timeout + login rate limit
- Careful client storage of tokens/sensitive data
- Safe production error responses
- No hard-delete of sensitive records (archive/void + reason)
- Audit log for important actions

Should apply as features land; Later items only when asked or after core auth is stable:
- CSRF/API token hardening, clinical vs finance isolation, export permission/log, dependency checks
- Later: MFA, selected field encryption, suspicious-login alerts

# Production-from-day-one (keep light, avoid rework)

- Build every screen as production UI from the start (shared layout, spacing, buttons, toast/modal). Do not ship “temporary ugly” UI that needs a later redesign.
- Prefer shared reusable components over one-off copies.
- Keep project structure clear: `frontend/` (React) and `backend/` (Laravel) unless user changes this.
- Use one consistent API success/error response shape across backend endpoints.
- Lists/pages should handle loading (skeleton), empty, and error states in a consistent simple way — no extra essays of helper text.
- Format money as PKR and dates/times consistently (Pakistan-friendly) via shared helpers when those fields appear.
- Print/PDF/Excel: only when building those features; use clean A4/receipt-ready layouts then — do not overbuild early.
- Do not add heavy process (big test suites, extra docs, changelogs) unless the user asks. Keep development smooth.

# GitHub / git

- Do not create commits unless the user explicitly asks.
- Do not push to remote unless the user explicitly asks and has provided remote access/link.
- Never commit secrets (`.env`, `Credentials.txt`, private keys).
- When the user provides the GitHub remote link/access and says to push, then push.

# Optimization tracker (auto-update)

- Source of truth: `OPTIMIZATION.md`
- Whenever an optimization item is implemented, partially applied, or deferred, update `OPTIMIZATION.md` status, “Where applied”, notes, and Summary counts.
- When the user asks what is done/remaining for optimization, read `OPTIMIZATION.md` and answer from it.
- Do not mark items Done unless actually implemented in code.

# Security tracker (auto-update)

- Source of truth: `SECURITY.md`
- Whenever a security item is implemented, partially applied, or deferred, update `SECURITY.md` status, “Where applied”, notes, and Summary counts.
- When the user asks what is done/remaining for security, read `SECURITY.md` and answer from it.
- Do not mark items Done unless actually implemented in code.

# Output format

- Use targeted code blocks or diffs, not full-file dumps.
- Keep root clean: avoid leftover launchers, legacy copies, or unused scaffolding.
